  AGPL section 13: Sealed runs the upstream image without modification, so the corresponding
  source is the upstream tag above. If Sealed ever patches Twenty, the patched source will be
  published next to this notice before the patched image is released.

  Enterprise-licensed files (decision D-019, escalated; this release is blocked until it is
  resolved): Twenty has no enterprise directory. Files whose first line is
  "/* @license Enterprise */" are under the Twenty.com Commercial License at the end of
  LICENSES/twenty-v2.52.0-LICENSE, and the official image compiles them into the same server and
  front-end bundles as the AGPL code, so no path can be left out. What that means here:
    - The enterprise-marked modules are in the image, and some of them run on every start with
      no key set: module setup (it reads the validity token from the database), entitlement
      checks, billing module wiring with billing off, and enterprise cron jobs that return early.
    - Features that need a valid enterprise key stay locked, because ENTERPRISE_KEY and
      ENTERPRISE_VALIDITY_TOKEN are never set: SSO, row-level permissions, audit logs, more than
      five workspaces. ENTERPRISE_API_URL is empty, IS_BILLING_ENABLED is false, and
      IS_CONFIG_VARIABLES_IN_DB_ENABLED is false, so no key can be stored from the admin panel.
      The egress policy allows no host, so twenty.com cannot be reached.
    - The Commercial License says that part of the software "may only be used in production"
      with an Enterprise subscription, and that otherwise "it is forbidden to copy, merge,
      publish, distribute, sublicense, and/or sell the Software". The release job mirrors the
      image to the catalog registry, which distributes those files.
  Because of the last point Sealed does not mirror or publish this app until the decision is
  made: either an image built from the tag with the enterprise-marked files removed, or a
  recorded exemption.

